Port patch 0035 - tell the graphics back end when the main pool gives out memory (main_pool_alloc_from_pool)
Why: the GPU renderer (shell/core/gpu_gles.c) keeps the colour surfaces the RDP drew into on the GPU and copies them back into game memory only when the game reads them
(a texture load) or when a surface of another shape replaces them. The console's RDP writes into RAM at once, so there the game simply overwrites that memory later. Here a
surface that was never read stays "newer than RAM" for ever, and when the pool had meanwhile given its memory to something else (decompressed assets, the pool's own block
headers) the late copy-back overwrote them: the block header of an unrelated pool block became a run of 0x60C7 (a framebuffer clear colour), the next main_pool_pop_state called a
"function" at 0x60C760C7 (trap: call through an empty function-table slot), and the rental icons of the team-selection menu were missing (measured: Stadium Select after the cup menu).
How: every pool allocation reports its range to __osHostPoolMemoryReused (src/ultra/hw_sp.c), which tells the renderer to drop the surfaces over that range WITHOUT copying them back:
fresh memory has no picture worth keeping. Nothing changes on the software renderer builds (the call does nothing there).

--- a/src/memory_main.c
+++ b/src/memory_main.c
@@ -4,6 sha256=49994b0a86811f8b331b8467f3c5a1c4c305569a8f9cfb04945558fdb98ceb62 @@
=3
+/* PORT (patch 0035): tells the graphics back end that this memory now belongs to something else (src/ultra/hw_sp.c). */
+extern void __osHostPoolMemoryReused(void* addr, u32 size);
=3
@@ -71,6 sha256=742f3588f7d08c7915cf837d244eca2b09038d9f1d485b49c651ae935fe4f2f4 @@
=3
+    if (addr != NULL) {
+        __osHostPoolMemoryReused((u8*)addr - sizeof(MainPoolBlock), size + sizeof(MainPoolBlock)); // PORT (patch 0035)
+    }
=3
